WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.589
Do we use social media

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.689
or does it use us?

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:11.189
That's the fundamental question

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:13.289
posed by artist Dave Cicirelli

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:14.969
in a series of works produced over

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.229
the past decade.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.389
They include "Fakebook," a memoir

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.459
of a fabricated, increasingly

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.619
unbelievable cross-country trip

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:24.749
that Cicirelli documented in real

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.609
time for his followers

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.539
on Facebook.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.459
Fake Banksy Sells Out:

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.769
A street sale of openly counterfeit

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.629
paintings in Central Park.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.209
"All Minus One," a graphic-novel

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.249
version of John Stuart Mill's ideas

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.649
on free speech and social conformity

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.509
done in collaboration with Jonathan

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.062
Haidt and Richard V.

00:00:44.063 --> 00:00:46.139
Reeves for Heterodox Academy.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.269
And The Infinity Cube,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.579
a mind blowing glass and mirrored

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.439
immersive installation that

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.389
challenges participants to

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:57.239
deny their urge to take a selfie.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.069
Born in 1983 and raised

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:00.959
in New Jersey, Cicirelli calls

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.939
himself an experiential artist

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.439
who forces the audience to

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.209
participate in the creation of

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.099
meaning. A longtime Reason reader

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.809
who is skeptical of both government

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.759
and corporate power, he's quite

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.589
possibly the only artist

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:15.359
who counts comic book legend Jack

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.459
Kirby and the late Senator Barry

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.530
Goldwater among his inspirations.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.829
Dave Cicirelli, thanks for talking

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:23.399
to Reason.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.839
Thanks for talking to me.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:26.699
You work in multiple

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:27.959
media.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.429
How do you define yourself as an

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:30.269
artist?

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.409
You know, I guess the term for what

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.299
I do is "experiential art," which is

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.099
really anything that involves

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.719
audience as the art where people

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.399
interact with it in a moment, in an

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.859
experience and their reaction

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:42.839
or their interactions with it is

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.969
as much part of the art as

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:45.959
the work that I do.

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.849
You published "Fakebook: A

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:50.249
True Story. Based on Actual Lies."

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.539
What was "Fakebook"?

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.239
So it was 2009, I

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.099
fictionalized my Facebook

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.199
profile and with the mix of

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.359
bad Photoshop and status updates,

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.369
had everyone I ever knew

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:03.359
believe that I was in this

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.279
disastrous cross-country

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:06.989
hike. And I kind of picked that

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.119
scenario for a couple reasons.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.799
One, is this the kind of thing

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:12.089
you've heard of people doing?

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.129
So it straddled that line of like,

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:15.449
is this real? Is it not?

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.309
What I kind of overlooked was

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:19.559
that people found that story

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:20.789
right when they couldn't look at a

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.529
spreadsheet any longer and they

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:24.359
logged on to Facebook for their, you

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.459
know, 32nd distraction.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.449
And then all of a sudden there's

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.519
this guy they kind of know

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.349
doing a thing they've always kind of

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:33.149
thought about doing, especially in

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:33.779
that moment.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:34.499
Right.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.179
So suddenly all these, like, thin,

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.009
loose connections

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:40.019
became very real and really

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.849
resonated. And people became so

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:43.739
emotionally invested in

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.689
the fact that I was brave enough

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:48.119
to do the thing I wasn't doing,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:50.219
that they became

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.079
very, very supportive in a way I

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.969
was really not prepared for.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.499
Take us through, you know, do a

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:56.339
spoiler alert.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:57.959
Like, what's the arc?

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.249
Yeah, I mean, it ends with a

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:00.929
confession. So, you know, I mean, I

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.029
start, I do it for six months and

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:05.069
it goes, you know,

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.109
through this whole series of kind of

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.969
interesting moments, you know, I

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.799
go to Amish country, fall

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:12.389
in love with this Amish girl.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.309
I go on the lam, she ends up

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:15.749
cheating on me, which was compelling

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.579
because everyone had an opinion, and

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:19.619
everybody was 100%

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:20.999
convinced they knew what happened,

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:22.499
what side to be on, all this sort of

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.089
stuff. You know, I was a drug mule

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:25.799
at one point. I was in a doomsday

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:26.579
cult.

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.469
Like, I just kept on daring and

00:03:28.470 --> 00:03:30.629
challenging people to find out.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:31.199
Right.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:32.489
You know, I mean, ultimately, when

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.439
the whole thing was confessed,

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.839
the reactions were very...they

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:38.409
almost had an arc, you know, which I

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:40.379
was, like, angry

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:41.669
then, like, actually that's kind of

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:42.119
funny.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:42.539
Yeah.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.029
Then like, wait, how did I possibly

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.979
believe any of this nonsense is kind

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:47.529
of interesting?

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:48.209
Yeah.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.039
Again, this was 2009 I did

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:51.989
this. This was before fake

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:53.849
news was a thing.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.739
Before influencers were

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:57.419
a term, but it was poking at the

00:03:57.420 --> 00:03:58.589
right questions.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.239
It was, you know, I mean, I had

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.279
somebody literally send

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.259
me a note, said I inspired

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.260
her to quit her job

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:07.469
and then, you know, immediately dry

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:08.969
heave because I don't have that kind

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.249
of, you know, weight.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.019
But, you know, that idea of the

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:14.309
influence and all this sort of

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.078
stuff, it was all right there at the

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:17.849
very budding of it.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.889
You know, does it pervert or debase

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.778
an authentic self or does it

00:04:21.779 --> 00:04:23.369
call into question, like, you know,

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.839
are you the person you are on

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.609
Instagram or are you the person

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:28.199
right here in this conversation or

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:28.829
in your studio?

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.329
I think that's the negotiation we're

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.799
all doing. You know, like, I'd say

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.209
the two things that it kind of gave

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.369
me unique perspectives on was

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:38.219
fake news and influencers.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.299
And I'll just say the influencer

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.579
one. First off, I felt very much

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.559
like I was shoveling coal

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.539
into, you know, into the

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.369
engine to make it keep moving

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:48.389
down the track.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:49.619
It's all disposable.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.609
It all goes away.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.679
And just I felt my entire

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.109
life was feeding this thing,

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.999
you know, that I was subservient

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.039
to it. And I think that's got to be

00:04:59.040 --> 00:04:59.989
what it's like.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:01.249
Talk about fake news.

00:05:01.250 --> 00:05:03.259
How does this anticipate

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:05.119
fake news and how are you

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:06.589
using that term?

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.149
And what does that say about our

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:09.619
kind of current moment?

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.569
Well, I think part of what

00:05:11.570 --> 00:05:13.699
I really got away from doing

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.559
this was I had a bunch of

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:17.179
people I would consider very smart

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:18.889
believing something very stupid.

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:19.012
Right.

00:05:19.013 --> 00:05:21.139
You know, so this kind of, this

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:23.389
general thumb nosing

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.249
attitude about fake news of like,

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:26.659
look at those rubes, they're too

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:27.979
dumb, they'll believe anything,

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:29.029
blah, blah, blah.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:32.359
I consider that nonsense because,

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:33.829
again, I had very smart people

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:36.019
believing pure nonsense.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:37.129
And the reason is, there's that

00:05:37.130 --> 00:05:38.029
emotional hook.

00:05:38.030 --> 00:05:39.409
You know, like I said before, I was

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:40.789
doing something everyone kind of

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:42.889
idly fantasizes about doing.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.869
So as long as that kind of emotional

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:46.819
resonance is there of whatever

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:48.889
that fake

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:50.869
story is, people will

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:52.939
go along with it a long way.

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:54.799
And let's talk about the,

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:56.419
the fake Banksy.

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.369
Sure. You know, so one of the real

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:00.319
iconic things he did was the Central

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:01.399
Park art sale.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:03.889
He set up a stall in Central Park

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:05.479
and they were selling original,

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:07.339
authenticated Banksy spray

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.969
art for $60.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.399
He had an old man selling it.

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.319
He sold there all day, like eight

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.489
or nine hours.

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.589
Sold, I think three customers,

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:17.141
seven pieces, three customers

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:19.759
then revealed that the following

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:21.889
day: I did this, you all missed

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:23.929
it, look, nobody cares about my art

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:25.399
if they aren't told that, they

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.569
should care about it—which is

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:28.559
brilliant and great and hilarious.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.469
So I called up Lance

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.509
Pilgrim. We've collaborated for

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:33.769
years and years on all kinds of

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:35.779
different things, and I was like,

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:38.299
Y"o, we got to redo this,"

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:39.739
but we're go to the exact same

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:40.549
place.

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.289
We're going to create the exact same

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.389
stall with the exact same works,

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:45.559
and we're going to sell them for the

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.479
exact same price, except we're going

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:49.579
to call it "fake Banksy."

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.409
We're going to tell everybody these

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:53.359
are not real Banksys and we're going

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:55.339
to even have legally notarized

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.769
certificates of inauthenticity

00:06:57.770 --> 00:06:59.659
decreeing legally,

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:00.919
these are not Banksy's.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:02.509
I know I want one just hearing

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:02.779
about.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:03.919
Oh, it was great. And we even

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:04.849
brought in an old man.

00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:06.259
We got Lance's father to be the

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:08.239
salesman and we sold out in less

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:09.469
than an hour. You know, I mean, my

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.149
favorite moment was wind knocked up

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.009
spray-painted rat off

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:15.079
our stand, hit a woman

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:16.699
in the head, and then she bought it

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:17.689
for $60.

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:18.709
You know.

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:19.879
People just didn't know what to

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:20.779
believe. They were just like,

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.219
energized, like when guy was, like,

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:23.779
sprinting to the ATM and being like,

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:24.649
don't sell, you know?

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:24.691
Yeah.

00:07:24.692 --> 00:07:26.569
I felt like we completed

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:27.859
Banksy statement.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:29.539
You know, his thing was, you know,

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:31.279
my artwork's all hyped.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:31.489
Yeah.

00:07:31.490 --> 00:07:33.169
And then we created his fake artwork

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:33.506
in the hype.

00:07:33.507 --> 00:07:33.517
Right.

00:07:33.518 --> 00:07:35.389
And then it sort

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.399
of became kind of a nice complement.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.169
What, what are the takeaways of,

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:40.489
like, the fake Banksy project?

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.559
Because one way of reading it is

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.479
you're pranking, you know,

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:45.979
the people who buy it or the art

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:47.209
world. But that's not where you're

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:47.959
going, right?

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:49.789
No, I mean, part of what I think is

00:07:49.790 --> 00:07:52.219
kind of fascinating about art,

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.049
in one sense, art is sort

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:55.969
of above value, but

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:57.799
an individual piece of art is pure

00:07:57.800 --> 00:07:58.858
capitalism. It's worth exactly

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:01.309
what someone's willing to pay for.

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:02.849
Right, at a given moment.

00:08:02.850 --> 00:08:04.669
That can that can change because Van

00:08:04.670 --> 00:08:06.799
Gogh, you know, alive,

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:07.789
not worth so much.

00:08:07.790 --> 00:08:08.689
Van Gogh dead?

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:10.579
Hey, you know, this is a bargain

00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:11.018
at any price.

00:08:11.019 --> 00:08:11.839
Right.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:13.699
Is, you know, is, am I

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:15.168
walking by nonsense Central

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:18.709
Park art stall.

00:08:18.710 --> 00:08:20.359
$60, maybe.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:22.129
Am I walking by a nonsense Central

00:08:22.130 --> 00:08:24.199
Park art stall after

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:26.419
Banksy, you know, made millionaires?

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:26.719
Yeah.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:28.679
You know, wasn't about

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:30.469
the spray art, as Banksy put it.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:32.298
It was about the, the consumers,

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:34.158
about us walking by or

00:08:34.159 --> 00:08:35.329
how we react to it.

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:37.158
Let's do a little biography here.

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:38.538
You grew up in Middletown, New

00:08:38.539 --> 00:08:38.869
Jersey.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:40.038
Middletown, New Jersey.

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:41.749
Intimately acquainted with as it's

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:43.369
my hometown as well.

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:45.889
Your interest in immersive

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:47.809
reality is that linked

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:49.729
to your biography in

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:51.979
any kind of obvious way that you're,

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:53.359
you think about.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:54.349
You know, I'd say I grew up in a

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:56.269
very practical kind of household, in

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.009
a very practical way.

00:08:58.010 --> 00:08:59.209
You know, I was kind of joke.

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:00.649
It was like doctor, lawyer,

00:09:00.650 --> 00:09:01.669
fireman—pick one.

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:03.649
You know, it's, like, basic, basic

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:04.650
jobs.

00:09:05.150 --> 00:09:07.099
I started taking

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.299
drawing lessons. I was, you know,

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:09.859
planning on being an illustrator, a

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.889
comic book illustrator specifically.

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:14.419
By this guy, Guy Dorian, still one

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:15.739
of my very good friends.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:17.599
And he taught me a couple really

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:20.059
good lessons, which is, one,

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:20.989
it's doable.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:22.759
Like, you know, like just proof of

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:24.679
example, you can make a living.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:26.209
And then the other was kind of how

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:28.129
to give yourself permission.

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:29.539
So like even around the opportunity

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:31.399
to write "Fakebook," I kind of

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:33.229
walked into a Barnes &amp; Noble and was

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:35.149
like, I can't possibly be the

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.189
worst writer with, you know,

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:38.239
on the shelves.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.189
I've always felt somewhat liberated

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:41.449
that I was doing a thing that didn't

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:41.874
need credentialing.

00:09:41.875 --> 00:09:42.875
Right.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:45.199
You know, in fact, I've always

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.569
kind of never even,

00:09:47.570 --> 00:09:49.219
I've never been tempted to get like

00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:51.199
a master's in art because I

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:53.059
just think you just do it, you

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.159
know, like everything I've,

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:56.839
every skill I have is because I had

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:58.699
some idea then figured

00:09:58.700 --> 00:09:59.999
out how to do it.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.969
And then, through trial and error

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:03.949
you learn, learn bunch

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:04.579
of shit.

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:05.929
At some point you became a Reason

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:06.809
reader as well.

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:07.179
Sure, yeah.

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:09.019
But is there a connection between a

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:10.969
kind of soft libertarian

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:13.219
sensibility and what you're doing?

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:14.239
Yeah, I think so.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.399
I grew up around smart Republicans

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:18.109
when I was a kid, which maybe is

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:19.639
part of this origin story.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.559
But my brother read me

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:23.689
Animal Farm when I was like,

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.323
you know, just barely, just old

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:27.049
enough to get it, right.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:27.499
Right.

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:28.909
So I kind of had that kind of

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:31.069
inoculation against

00:10:31.070 --> 00:10:32.239
orthodoxy.

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:34.099
So then when I went to college, I

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:35.419
kind of counterprogrammed, and I

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:37.519
read things like "Wealth of Nations"

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.259
and I read Barry Goldwater's

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:40.639
"Conscious of the Conservative" was

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:42.019
the thing that probably shifted me

00:10:42.020 --> 00:10:43.909
most libertarian,

00:10:43.910 --> 00:10:45.379
where I kind of, that, that idea of

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:47.659
like it's we're not obsessed

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:49.759
with material gain, which

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.709
is the criticism, often.

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:53.119
It's, like, actually it's the human

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:55.369
spirit. It's this idea of doing,

00:10:55.370 --> 00:10:56.899
you know, of what your passion is,

00:10:56.900 --> 00:10:58.039
what you're thinking is.

00:10:58.040 --> 00:10:59.579
And I read Federalist Papers, all

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:01.189
this kind of stuff. So I always kind

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:02.329
of had that instinct.

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:04.429
What I kind of appreciate about

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.259
Reason, I think

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:08.359
maybe libertarians general position

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:10.789
in the culture war is they actually

00:11:10.790 --> 00:11:12.859
have the luxury of being

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:14.809
consistent, you know, with their

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:16.789
ideology or,

00:11:16.790 --> 00:11:18.649
you know, not be so vetted

00:11:18.650 --> 00:11:21.589
in every single win and loss

00:11:21.590 --> 00:11:23.239
that there was, like, a consistent,

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.269
realistic and principled

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:28.489
conversation and perspective

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:29.749
that was kind of coming through in

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:30.409
pushing through.

00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.269
A project of yours that I

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:34.249
want to ask you about.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:35.509
And this is something you did with

00:11:35.510 --> 00:11:37.579
Jonathan Haidt and Heterodox

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:39.409
Academy and with Richard Reeves,

00:11:39.410 --> 00:11:41.479
the Brookings Institution scholar.

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:44.119
It's part of John Stuart Mill's

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:46.399
"On Liberty" arguments

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:48.709
about, you know, kind of free speech

00:11:48.710 --> 00:11:51.319
and or free thinking, really,

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:53.239
called "All Minus One." Can

00:11:53.240 --> 00:11:54.829
you talk a little bit about this

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:56.779
project and how it fits

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:58.789
in to your artscape?

00:11:58.790 --> 00:11:59.899
Yeah, I mean, there was A, it was

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:01.309
just kind of an honor to work on it.

00:12:01.310 --> 00:12:03.139
I was, you know, admirer

00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:04.609
of John's work, and I kind of

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:06.589
reached out and he's like, I

00:12:06.590 --> 00:12:07.369
like what you do.

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:08.899
This is what I do.

00:12:08.900 --> 00:12:10.729
Let me know if what I do is

00:12:10.730 --> 00:12:12.469
useful to what you do.

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:14.359
And, you know, John tends to have an

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:16.189
answer, you know, like, so

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:17.839
yeah, I mean, I read "On Liberty" is

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:19.429
part of that counter-program back in

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.349
the day, but the idea of it was

00:12:21.350 --> 00:12:23.719
as kind of cultural censorship

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:25.969
is taking rise and people are,

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:27.859
you know, starting to view words as

00:12:27.860 --> 00:12:29.809
violence. And, you know, this kind

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:32.059
of base instinct to silence

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:34.189
was sort of reemerging

00:12:34.190 --> 00:12:36.349
in our, in our culture as a

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:38.089
puritanical force.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.699
It was, you know, this work is

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:41.779
really relevant.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:43.759
The idea was parse it down,

00:12:43.760 --> 00:12:45.229
make it really digestible and

00:12:45.230 --> 00:12:47.149
distribute it freely to college

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:49.069
campuses and high schools as part

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:50.899
of a curriculum effort and make the

00:12:50.900 --> 00:12:51.979
work accessible.

00:12:51.980 --> 00:12:53.419
And then it was my job again to kind

00:12:53.420 --> 00:12:55.129
of distill even further into these

00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:57.079
images that would really evoke and

00:12:57.080 --> 00:12:58.129
drive it further.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:12:59.509
And to me, it's, you know, the

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:02.029
general idea here is the most

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:03.919
central to anything to really should

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.869
be all creative work, which

00:13:05.870 --> 00:13:07.939
is, you know, the freedom to poke

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:09.349
and ask questions.

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:10.549
I mean, that's, that's what it's

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.049
about. It's, you know, maybe you're

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:14.449
right or maybe you need to fold

00:13:14.450 --> 00:13:16.729
a piece of other perspectives

00:13:16.730 --> 00:13:18.259
and other wisdoms tp create the

00:13:18.260 --> 00:13:20.209
truth. And sort of this shifting

00:13:20.210 --> 00:13:22.039
of, of this idea

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:24.229
of truth, of being something you own

00:13:24.230 --> 00:13:26.419
versus truth being the end

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:28.279
result of a process of finding it

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:29.029
out.

00:13:29.030 --> 00:13:30.949
So let's talk about The Infinity

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:32.779
Cube, which is your kind

00:13:32.780 --> 00:13:35.089
of current main focus?

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:36.829
Yeah, so I really like to think of

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:38.419
it more of a canvas than a single

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:40.339
piece of art, but obviously

00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:41.719
I'm not the first person to point

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:42.739
mirrors at each other.

00:13:42.740 --> 00:13:44.959
You got Yayoi Kusama, you've got

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:46.879
Hall of Mirrors, you've got

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:48.439
your mom's bathroom in the eighties.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:49.729
It's been done.

00:13:49.730 --> 00:13:51.019
But this is unique in a couple of

00:13:51.020 --> 00:13:52.759
ways. Number one, you'll notice it's

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:54.289
motion, it's video-based.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:56.059
It's kinetic, it's moving, and that

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:57.889
sort of creates a kaleidoscopic

00:13:57.890 --> 00:13:58.849
experience.

00:13:58.850 --> 00:14:00.709
Two, you'll notice not only is

00:14:00.710 --> 00:14:02.539
there a screen, but you see past the

00:14:02.540 --> 00:14:04.039
screen. So when you look through it,

00:14:04.040 --> 00:14:05.599
you get that kind of uninterrupted

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:07.699
depth. So it's just like just

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:08.869
reflections.

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:09.659
Can we go inside?

00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:10.660
Absolutely.

00:14:14.570 --> 00:14:16.459
And when you're inside,

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.769
you can't see outside.

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:19.249
Correct.

00:14:19.250 --> 00:14:20.719
Well, like we are, I mean, I'm

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:22.609
saying a perfect reflection of us,

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:24.559
but they're seeing

00:14:24.560 --> 00:14:26.389
you and me chatting.

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:27.529
They're seeing us chatting, and

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:28.759
they're seeing all the reflections

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:29.899
around us.

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:32.539
So our image is being reproduced

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:34.609
and replicated and degrading

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:35.779
in all directions.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:37.669
Is this a commentary on

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:39.739
like we live in a world now

00:14:39.740 --> 00:14:41.149
because of social media or

00:14:41.150 --> 00:14:43.219
technology where there

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:45.139
are a million versions of

00:14:45.140 --> 00:14:47.059
us everywhere.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:48.349
I mean yeah.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:49.309
And they're all a little bit

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:49.849
different.

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:51.829
I think the metaphorical quality

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:54.109
of this space is exactly

00:14:54.110 --> 00:14:56.029
that. You step in, you put

00:14:56.030 --> 00:14:58.279
yourself on display to an audience

00:14:58.280 --> 00:14:59.059
you can't see.

00:14:59.060 --> 00:14:59.899
But they're out there.

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:00.539
But they're out there.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:01.549
But you forget that.

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:02.629
You forget that.

00:15:02.630 --> 00:15:04.489
Your relationship to the work or to

00:15:04.490 --> 00:15:06.349
anything really is, Are you

00:15:06.350 --> 00:15:08.269
in front of it or is it in front of

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:08.599
you?

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:08.989
Mm hmm.

00:15:08.990 --> 00:15:10.669
You know, that's the real tension.

00:15:10.670 --> 00:15:12.109
And if you put yourself in front of

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:13.819
it, then you kind of make everything

00:15:13.820 --> 00:15:15.199
kind of submissive to your own

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:17.239
vanity. You want your trophy?

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:18.739
I'm at the Sistine Chapel.

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:20.689
Let me put my face

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:22.189
in front of Michelangelo's work,

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:24.139
because Lord knows my face

00:15:24.140 --> 00:15:25.169
is more important.

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:26.509
Like, come on. But if you let

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:28.519
yourself sit and let yourself absorb

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:30.469
and have it be in front of you

00:15:30.470 --> 00:15:32.149
and in this case, wrapped all around

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:34.219
you, then you do actually

00:15:34.220 --> 00:15:35.959
kind of transcend that moment.

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:37.099
And what I can do with the lighting

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:38.599
trick is that if you give in and you

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.519
want to take your picture, we

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:42.499
make that choice that you made very

00:15:42.500 --> 00:15:44.479
real by changing the lighting.

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:45.619
And then all of a sudden the whole

00:15:45.620 --> 00:15:47.539
audience can see you and you're

00:15:47.540 --> 00:15:49.819
exposed, which is what you wanted.

00:15:49.820 --> 00:15:51.469
But it's not what you wanted, but it

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:52.609
is what you wanted.

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:54.289
And it's sort of that tension is

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:56.149
where I think this thing can

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:57.679
really transcend just being kind of

00:15:57.680 --> 00:16:00.349
a visually beautiful and cool

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:03.049
experience and actually kind of

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:04.939
really deliver on that idea of

00:16:04.940 --> 00:16:06.259
experiential art and making the

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:08.209
audience that the

00:16:08.210 --> 00:16:09.528
piece, the center of it.

00:16:09.529 --> 00:16:11.959
The Infinity Cube has gone on tour,

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:13.909
you've been like the

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:15.649
baseball Major League Baseball All

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:17.509
Star Game, the Juno Awards,

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:19.789
the Canadian Grammy Awards.

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:22.099
Why are these large organizations

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:23.809
paying you to drag this thing out to

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:24.919
their events?

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:26.509
Kind of the exact opposite of what I

00:16:26.510 --> 00:16:27.469
want to prove.

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:28.999
They want really cool pictures.

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:31.249
If Donald Trump walked into

00:16:31.250 --> 00:16:33.169
the Infinity Cube, would he

00:16:33.170 --> 00:16:35.149
explode or would it explode?

00:16:35.150 --> 00:16:36.049
I don't know.

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:37.489
That's a good question.

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:39.409
I mean, I know there would be a rift

00:16:39.410 --> 00:16:41.000
in the time–space continuum.

00:16:42.890 --> 00:16:44.599
Yeah, it just depends how

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:45.769
comfortable it is looking at

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:46.429
himself.

00:16:46.430 --> 00:16:47.569
I think it's very comfortable.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:48.069
Might be.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:49.489
Yeah. Yeah. Well, you know what?

00:16:49.490 --> 00:16:50.629
We're going to leave it there.

00:16:50.630 --> 00:16:51.709
We've been talking with Dave

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:52.639
Cicirelli.

00:16:52.640 --> 00:16:53.959
Thanks for talking to Reason.

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:55.100
Thanks for talking to me.

